Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 10:56:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 061/154] feat: walk tabs by recency under a held Ctrl-Tab
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Nothing on the C++ side has kept a recency list since the Swift cut deleted the
Swift model, so Ctrl-Tab has fallen through to stock strip cycling. The list
and the walk now live in Rust; this hook only asks for the next tab and shows
it, falling through to super when there is no controller or nowhere to go.

A cycle walks a SNAPSHOT taken at the first press and does not reorder until it
commits, or two presses would land back where they started - the toggle the design
forbids. It commits on Control's release: a timeout has no correct value, and a
second key adds a mode the user can forget they are in. Release is watched with
one process-wide flagsChanged monitor, refcounted down from the
one-delegate-per-window fan-out an earlier fix was bitten by, plus the resignKey and
resignActive endings the sidebar scrub needed - filtered to the
window that owns the walk, since the activation itself makes other windows
resign key.

A second fix travels with it because it edits the same function: Cmd+, and
Cmd+Opt+I stop being claimed here. They were claimed because both chords
reached prePerform and never postPerform on an HTML surface; the preceding
commit fixed the reason, so the claims - and ToggleDevToolsForVisibleTab, whose
only caller they were - come out.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
